| 7 | an all-uppercase word of two or more letters (`BOS`, `GPT`, `BLK`), other than a two-letter repeat such as `AA`, is lexed as an **operator word**, not an identifier | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | `basic/lexical-structure.tex`, "Operator Tokens" | none | blinded | siblings: 193. `Operator prefix BOS is not defined.` Workaround: rename (`Blk`). The spec's operator word has at least two different letters; the parser's test is looser, so `AAA` is an operator too (row 193, `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nodes_util/NodeUtil.java:1466-1485`). In an APL sub-language name, `BLK ← 4 ⋄ BLK` is a `Syntax Error` where `Blk ← 4 ⋄ Blk` runs, and single capitals (`Q`, `K`) are fine: `explorations/apl/rung-5/v06_caps.fss` holds the working half, its `.out.0` (in the parent of `1ee3b0bc5`) the error. Probe `explorations/fable-review-probes/RvwNameKeywords.fss`, in the parent of `e879b6a44`. full text: history, row 7 |
| 133 | a postfix operator declaration whose symbol is an exponent operator, `opr (self)^T` or `opr (A: Mat)^T`, is a `Syntax Error` in an API, while the same declaration parses in a component | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/appendices/grammars/concrete-syntax.tex:737-738`, "Parameters" | none | run-b2, run-c2, run-c3 | The component rule has `(Op / ExponentOp / caret …)` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Parameter.rats:141-142`); the API rule lacks the second `/` (`parser/Parameter.rats:171-172`; the comments at `:115`, `:159` have the spec's alternation), so its postfix must be an `ExponentOp` then a caret, which `^T` (`exponentOp`, `parser/Symbol.rats:104-106`) never is. A plain postfix `!` in an API parses and runs; `^T^` is refused later: `The operator, ^, is always an infix operator but it is declared to be postfix.` The error sits at column 1 of the next line. Probes `explorations/run-c3/probes/g156_api_postfix/`, in the parent of `a0a0c7c99`. full text: history, row 133 |
| 144 | a call immediately followed by a postfix or superscript operator, `onehot(ids, vocabSize)^T`, is a static error by the specification, as `f()[i]` is (row 4) | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (walk) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/operators/juxtameaning.tex:156-162`, "Juxtaposition" | none | run-c | siblings: 4, 158. `Syntax Error: a function argument should not be immediately followed by a non-expression element.` at the `^`: walk's postfix branch takes the tight juxtaposition (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/Evaluator.java:969-971`) and refuses the call (`Evaluator.java:1322-1326`). Workarounds: bind first, `oh = onehot(…); (oh^T) dX` (the probe's working half prints `(1) bound first: (a^T).sizes = 2 3`), or a dotted method call, `onehot(ks, 5).t()` (row 301). Probe `explorations/run-c/probes/g144_callsuper.fss`, in the parent of `4ebf80aec`. full text: history, row 144 |
| 158 | walk refuses a parenthesised call followed by `^T`, `(onehot(ks, 5))^T dx` or `((onehot(ks, 5))^T) dx`, as row 144's call-then-postfix error, though the grammar makes a parenthesised expression a `Primary` that an `ExponentOp` may follow | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/operator-app.tex:29-32`, "Operator Applications"; `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/operators/juxtameaning.tex:156-162`, "Juxtaposition" | none | run-c2, run-c3 | siblings: 144. the error of row 144, its span on the call, not the parentheses: walk's postfix branch tests only for a tight juxtaposition (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/Evaluator.java:969-971`), not for parentheses, and refuses it (`Evaluator.java:1322-1326`). Workarounds: `transpose(onehot(ks, 5)) dx`, `(onehot(ks, 5)).t() dx`, or bind first (`lift_g4b` beside the probe). Probes `explorations/run-c/probes/q04f_callsuper.fss` (3), `explorations/run-c/probes/q04a_varargs.fss` (5), in the parent of `4ebf80aec`; `explorations/run-c3/probes/lift_g4.fss`, in the parent of `a0a0c7c99`. full text: history, row 158 |
| 193 | a word of **two or more uppercase letters** cannot name a nonterminal, being an operator word, and the implementation's rule is looser than the spec's “at least two different letters”: `AAA` is an operator in the implementation and an identifier in the spec | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `basic/lexical-structure.tex`, "Operator Tokens" | none | apl rung 2 | siblings: 7, 257. A nonterminal called `WE` is a bare `Syntax Error` at the name; workaround: `WEx`. `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Symbol.rats:117-118` lexes `OpName = id &{NodeUtil.validOp(opn)}`; `nodes_util/NodeUtil.java:1466-1485` is the rule: length ≥ 2, all uppercase or underscore, no leading or trailing `_`, and only `AA`-style two-character repeats and `AA_…` excluded. The eleven-name script accepts `AA Ab AbC A W2 AB2` and rejects `WE AB ABC AAA A_B`. Probes `explorations/apl/v1-probes/p12a_oprword.sh`, `p12_name.out.0`, in the parent of `1ee3b0bc5`, run on the retired first library (`explorations/apl/v1/base` at `0ff5eae27~1`). full text: history, row 193 |
| 257 | in a grammar api a bare `[_]` character class is a Syntax Error at the character and must be written `` [`_] ``, and a closed-set name with an underscore must stand above its prefix in the set | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl microgpt rung | siblings: 193, 250. `_` is one of the macro language's SpecialChars (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Syntax.rats:395-398`, as row 250), so a bare `[_]` fails. The order matters because `_` is outside `[A:Za:z0:9]` and the `NOT` terminator succeeds after the prefix: `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi:610-617` has `rmsn_b` above `rmsn`, with a comment saying why. A closed-set name may carry a digit after a capital (`B1 Lr0 M0 X1`) and an underscore (`rmsn_b`), so row 193 bars only words of two or more capitals. Probes `explorations/apl/microgpt/probes/X01Syn.fsi`, `x01_names.out.0` (the bare class's failure), in the parent of `1ee3b0bc5`. full text: history, row 257 |
| 286 | a **template that writes the host's caret drops its right operand silently**: `<[ (1.0 (l)) ^ (r) ]>` expands to `1.0 (l)` with no diagnostic, so `apl⦇ 10*10 ⦈` is `10.0` and `apl⦇ 2*3 ⦈` is `2.0` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 4b | In the template translator; `^` is also the type-application and postfix-operator character (rows 133, 144, 158). To reproduce, write the `*` rule at `explorations/apl/mg/AplMgSyntax.fsi:234` as `<[ (1.0 (l)) ^ (r) ]>`. The causal mask came out `-10` instead of `-1E10`, which moved every loss from the seventh digit (step 1 `3.3659665281147144` against the golden `3.365966947584851`). Workaround, exact: call a glue function, `pow(b: RR64, e: ZZ32): RR64 = b^e` (`explorations/apl/mg/AplMg.fss:37`), as line 234 does. Rule for a sub-language: no host caret in a template. Checks `explorations/apl/mg/checks/model_run.out`, `.out.1`, in the parent of `1ee3b0bc5`. full text: history, row 286 |
